.TH "PyAssembler" "1" "April 2026" "PyAssembler Use Manuals"
.SH NAME
pyassembler \- A C\-written python files assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
pyassembler <\f[I]directory name\f[R]> [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.PP
See \f[B]OPTIONS\f[R] below
.SH DESCRIPTION
PyAssembler is a small C program that aims to assemble all python files
from a folder into one unique python file.
It is written in C and uses POSIX system calls for the most part
.PP
PyAssembler will recursively read all the files from a given directory,
and keep only the python files, remembering imports and filtering only
standard/non\-custom files imports, to put them in the output.
The output can be controlled using the options, such as
\f[CR]\-\-start\-of\-file\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-end\-of\-file\f[R].
It will, by default, create a comment to signal the start of the
imports, the end of the imports, the start of a file and the end of a
file
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src
.EE
.PP
If no \f[I]directory name\f[R] is specified, it will throw an error.
If it does not exist, it will not be created and will fail.
.PP
The program follows all symlinks, directories and files.
.PP
If no output file is specified, it defaults to
\f[CR]./output/main.py\f[R].
The output can be changed using \f[CR]\-o\f[R] flag
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-o dist/out.py
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
\-h, \-\-help : Displays the help page if it is found.
The same behavior is executed if no arguments is given
.IP
.EX
pyassembler \-h
pyassembler
pyassembler src \-\-no\-comments \-o dist/out.py \-\-help
.EE
.PP
\-\-version : Displays program version.
This option overrides any other command, exept \-\-help
.IP
.EX
pyassembler \-\-version
pyassembler src \-\-no\-comments \-\-version
.EE
.PP
\-o \f[I]OUTPUT PATH\f[R] : Specify the output path.
If the file does not exist, it will be created.
If it exists, it will be truncated.
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-o dist/out.py
pyassembler src \-o build/output.py
.EE
.PP
\-v, \-\-verbose : Enables verbosity if found.
It might do nothing, if the binary has been compiled with the
\f[CR]DISABLE_VERBOSE=1\f[R] flag, but the verbosity is available by
default.
If you want, you can compile your own \f[B]pyassembler\f[R] binary, with
the \f[CR]DISABLE_VERBOSE=1\f[R] flag, so the checks are removed and
gives a slight performance boost (not much)
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-v
pyassembler src \-\-verbose
.EE
.PP
\-\-end\-of\-file \f[I]PRELUDE\f[R] : The comment message to be
displayed before the file name in the assembled file.
By default, if comments are enabled, this will be written : #
PyAssembler::end: /path/of/the/source/file.py.
The message can be modified, but the file name will be appened at the
end.
Enabling \f[CR]\-\-no\-comments\f[R] removes this features, by removing
all comments
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-end\-of\-file \(dqEnd of file: \(dq
pyassembler src \-\-end\-of\-file \(dq========[END]======[FILE]:\(dq
.EE
.PP
\-\-fullpaths : Shows fullpaths in comments of start and end (the ones
that can be modified by \f[CR]\-\-start\-of\-file\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-end\-of\-file\f[R]).
Enabling \f[CR]\-\-no\-comments\f[R] removes this features, by removing
all comments, and thus the file names, shortened or longened by
\f[CR]\-\-fullpaths\f[R]
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-fullpaths
pyassembler src \-\-start\-of\-file \(dqStart of (full): \(dq \-\-end\-of\-file \(dqEnd of (full): \(dq
.EE
.PP
\-\-last\-file \f[I]FILE PATH\f[R] : Specify the last file to be
assembled.
This will tell \f[B]PyAssembler\f[R] to remember the file at
\f[I]directory name/FILE PATH\f[R] if it encounters it during the
recursion, and treat it as last file.
It is useful to have one file at the end of the assembled file.
The given \f[I]directory name\f[R] will be prepended before \f[I]FILE
PATH\f[R] to, so it is a relative path name to the input directory
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-last\-file main.py
.EE
.PP
\-\-max\-newlines \f[I]NEW LINES COUNT\f[R] : Specify the maximum
numbers of consecutives empty lines.
It might be useful if \f[CR]\-\-no\-comments\f[R] is specified, as it
can create unwanted blank lines between files.
Default value is 1
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-max\-newlines 1
pyassembler src \-\-max\-newlines 0 \-\-no\-comments
.EE
.PP
\-\-no\-comments : Disable all assembler comments, being : \f[B]start of
files\f[R], \f[B]end of files\f[R], \f[B]start of imports list\f[R] and
\f[B]end of imports list\f[R].
If is is enabled, the \f[CR]\-\-start\-of\-file\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-end\-of\-file\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-fullpaths\f[R] flags have no
effect
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-no\-comments
.EE
.PP
\-\-start\-of\-flie \f[I]PRELUDE\f[R] : The comment message to be
displayed before the file name in the assembled file.
By default, if comments are enabled, this will be written : #
PyAssembler::Start: /path/of/the/source/file.py.
The message can be modified, but the file name will be appened at the
end anyway.
Enabling \f[CR]\-\-no\-comments\f[R] removes this features, by removing
all comments
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-end\-of\-file \(dqStart of file: \(dq
pyassembler src \-\-end\-of\-file \(dq========[START]======[FILE]:\(dq
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
The following command will scan all files from \f[CR]src\f[R], treat the
file \f[CR]src/runtime/main.py\f[R] in last, allowing 2 consecutive
lines, and showing fullpaths in comments, and write the output in
\f[CR]./dist/out.py\f[R]
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-last\-file runtime/main.py \-\-max\-newlines 2 \-o dist/out.py
.EE
.PP
The following commands will scan all files from \f[CR]src\f[R], will
verbosely tell what it is doing, writting no comments, treat
\f[CR]src/main.py\f[R] in last, and write the output in
\f[CR]./output/main.py\f[R]
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-last\-file main.py \-\-no\-comments
pyassembler src \-\-last\-file main.py \-\-no\-comments \-o output/main.py
.EE
.PP
The following command will use (almost) all parameters
.IP
.EX
pyassembler src \-\-last\-file main.py \-\-start\-of\-file \(dqSTART OF: \(dq \-\-end\-of\-file \(dqEND OF: \(dq \-\-fullpaths \-\-max\-newlines 2 \-v
.EE
